s sense of  unfairness what is there do you think  that this is a struck you know we've  we've reached a new part of history and  there's economists in the u.s. they talk  about this well welcome to two percent  annual north if we're lucky the years of  3% 4% 5% are over so that we really need  to manage the the scant resources that  we have is that myopic or I think we  thought like that in the 70s in Britain  yeah that it was over it was over the  phrase was managed to decline it's a  question of how civilized away can we do  this you know  and there were apparently rational  reasons for thinking that you know we  had constant strikes we had double-digit  inflation that we had it also broken up  right prices and incomes policies I mean  a Conservative government passing laws  and what you were allowed to earn and  what you were allowed to set your prices  had it was a completely reasonable thing  to think we're finished but of course we  didn't realize then that we were on the  verge of a great national Renaissance  under Margaret Thatcher  so I actually I'm very optimistic about  this country and about the long-term  future of it I think the the  individualistic and dispersed political  and legal institutions of the u.s. it  very very well for prosperity in this  century and I see a great future with a  weak driverless cars and advances in  biotech and 3d printing and all sorts of  things based on innovation because the  innovators were always several steps  ahead of regulators they're always  clever perhaps talk a bit about both in  Europe as well as in the United States  there's there is a fear that with a kind  of  populist uprising against established  powers which I think we would agree is a  good thing  nativist sentiments racialist sentiments  come in and we already see I mean on the  Left people hold on to racial categories  ethnic categories gender categories very  much so they don't want to let those go  and on the right you're seeing that in  the United States the alt-right talks  about a white nationalism that really  hasn't been heard from since the late  60s or early 70s in the United States is  that a concern for you either in England  or in the US or across the developed  world where it seems like you know  things are breaking down along racial  ethnic lines in a way that's troubling  I'm not sure how true that is it's it's  natural to become alarmed about people  saying extreme things even if their  numbers of time grow if you spend your  life on Twitter you think the old right  is a very big deal but how many times do  you hear any normal person raising it in  the real world rather than virtually  right funnily enough I just had a  meeting with the behavioral psychologist  Jonathan Haight brilliant brilliant  writer who I think I think his work is  at the most it's the most important  cutting-edge work being done in terms of  understanding what drives our political  sympathies and his point was that  actually most of this sentiment is not  about race or categorization by you know  ethnicity gender and so on but is about  a sense of being insulted having your  values traduced in other words being  called a racist all the time and that  that's created a backlash from people  who are not in any sense races now of  course there is a minority of racist  numbskulls in your country in mine and  in every other event but that but I  think we should keep a sense of  proportion the backlash is not really  driven by the people who have jumped  onto it it's it's driven by a sense that  a elites used a crony ax stand  co-operative system to enrich themselves  at public expense and then be that  having done that they turned around and  spat on the values of the people who'd  bailed them out  for Trump and you you've written  recently into Washington Washington  Examiner not that you are warming up to  Trump and that he's your D thawing  towards him yeah  what are what are the early indicators  that will make you think okay this guy  is heading in the right direction word  now this we got a you know there's a  real problem well I guess it by the time  people watch this video they'll have a  better idea of what the administration  then as I'm talking to you but obviously  that's going to be the first earlier  sign from hearing the speculation I do  find quite encouraging actually I think  is some really good names you're out  there you know I was never that worried  about I think some of the hysteria it's  not just sort of self-indulgent and and  solipsistic it it kind of totally misses  the point about the American  Constitution your founders had someone  very like Donald Trump in mind when they  designed all these checks and balances  the capacity of Hillary Clinton and like  Hillary Clinton exactly even the most  Caligula and president is constrained my  guess is that there is basically a kind  of anti Trump majority in both houses  now there were lots of Republicans  elected who didn't really hide their  views of him and that that provides a  constraint and I think he you know maybe  I'm wrong and we'll see how he works out  but I mean he is somebody who at least  understands about deal-making and  understands about when a deal can't be  done and how you should then make the  best of it all right well we will leave  it there we've been talking with Daniel  Hannan he is a member of the European  Parliament and he was one of the main  people behind brexit co-founder of the  vote leave group Daniel thanks for  talking too much for recent TV I'm Nick  Gillespie  [Music]
 

